;; TEST-EXPECT: PASS
;; TEST-WITH[--hardware-profile=bmg --ir-target=spv]
;; SKIP-DEFAULT-PASS
;; TEST-HOIST[L0]: validate-l0-host-run
;; HOIST-HARDWARE-PROFILE: bmg
;; HOIST-EXPECT: BUFFER out: 80 1 2 3
;; SKIP-WITH[--differentiate]: "atomic-op! is not differentiable -- see errors/14"
;;
;; ENDEAVOUR 175 -- atomic-op!, the UNARY sibling of atomic-binop!.
;;
;;   (atomic-op! location op-f)
;;
;; Applies a unary #(T => T) to the value at LOCATION and stores the result, returning the value
;; that was there before.  A bounded CAS retry loop, exactly like atomic-binop! -- the only
;; difference is the arity of the function and that there is no value argument at all.
;;
;; THE DOC MARKED THIS IMPLEMENTED AND IT WAS NOT.  atomic-excerpts.md carries a tick for both
;; atomic-binop! and atomic-op!; neither existed before 2026-09-22.  That is worth a line here
;; because a wrong tick is worse than a missing one: it stops anybody looking.
;;
;; plus-ten IS THE DOC'S OWN EXAMPLE, kept deliberately rather than reusing spec 36's multiply.
;; Eight threads each add ten to a slot starting at 0, so the result is 80 -- and because the
;; operation is applied once per thread, 80 is only reachable if every one of the eight CAS loops
;; succeeded.  A single lost update reads 70, which is unmistakable rather than plausible.
;;
;; WHY THE ORDER DOES NOT MATTER even though eight threads race: every thread applies the SAME
;; unary function once, so the final value is f composed with itself eight times over the initial
;; value, and function composition does not care which thread went first.  That is what makes the
;; expectation deterministic.  (It is also why the DERIVATIVE is not -- see errors/14: each thread's
;; adjoint depends on which rung of that composition chain it occupied, which differs per run.)
;;
;; THE ACCUMULATOR IS out[0], which starts at 0 and is fine here: addition's identity is 0, so the
;; pre-fill does not perturb the answer.  Contrast spec 36, where multiplication forced the use of
;; out[1] because a product seeded at 0 stays 0.

(def-type vec-t (vector float :address-space :global :align :compact))

;; The design doc's own example function.
(def-function plus-ten (a)
  (declare #'(float => float))
  (+ a 10.0))

(def-kernel atomic_op_unary (&out out)
  (declare #'(&out vec-t => nil)
           (global-size :set-to 8)
           (local-size  :set-to 8))
  (atomic-op! (~ out 0) #'plus-ten))
